"""nexus-security-status — the security state of this system, as JSON.

    nexus-security-status [--section NAME[,NAME…]] [--pretty]

One read-only report that the Security Center and the Control Center render.
Every field is the result of an actual check made when the program runs:
nothing is cached, estimated or scored. A check that cannot be performed
reports {"available": false, "reason": "..."} instead of a guess.

Sections:
    firewall     nftables: NEXUS table loaded? start at boot? opened ports
    apparmor     kernel module state, profile counts by mode, confined processes
    encryption   LUKS: is the root filesystem / home / swap on encrypted storage
    secureboot   firmware type, Secure Boot and setup mode, kernel lockdown, shim/MOK
    updates      pending package upgrades, security upgrades, restart required
    listening    listening TCP/UDP sockets with owning process
    connections  established connections with owning process
    services     running system services
    logins       current sessions, recent logins, failed authentication attempts
    audit        auditd state, loaded rules, event counts by rule key
    hardening    kernel hardening settings compared with the NEXUS baseline,
                 CPU vulnerability mitigations, boot-loader refresh state
    recommendations  findings derived from the sections above

It needs root to see everything (other users' sockets, AppArmor profile list,
the nftables ruleset, audit and authentication logs). Graphical tools run it
through pkexec; the polkit action org.nexus.security.read-status lets an
active local session do so without a password prompt because the program is
strictly read-only and takes no input beyond the section names.
"""
